Data skew occurs when your data is unevenly distributed across the partitions in your cluster. 
Because a single Spark task processes exactly one partition, skew means one worker node gets crushed with 99% of the data
while the other nodes finish instantly and sit idle.

Technical Breakdown :- 

The Symptom: If you look at the Spark UI, you'll see a stage with 200 tasks. 199 tasks finish in 3 seconds,
but the last task takes 45 minutes, or crashes completely with an Out Of Memory (OOM) error.

The Cause: Imagine joining a global sales table on country_code. If 90% of your sales are in the 'US',
Spark hashes the key 'US' and sends all 90% of those records to a single partition on a single node.
The cluster's parallelism is completely destroyed.

Buisness Context :- 

Data skew is the silent killer of cloud budgets and data SLAs. When one node is struggling for hours,
you are still paying the hourly compute rate for the other 49 nodes in the cluster that are doing absolutely nothing.
Fixing skew transforms an unreliable, expensive 2-hour pipeline into a stable 5-minute job

Design Decision :-

Historically, we fixed this manually using Salting. We would append a random number (the 'salt') to the skewed key (e.g., changing 'US' to 'US_1', 'US_2', up to 'US_10').
This artificially broke the massive partition into 10 smaller chunks that distributed evenly across the cluster.
We would then replicate the join table 10 times, perform the join, and strip the salt off.
Today, however, if I am running Spark 3.0 or later, I just ensure Adaptive Query Execution (AQE) is turned on (spark.sql.adaptive.enabled = true).
AQE dynamically detects skew at runtime and automatically splits the skewed partitions for me, completely eliminating the need for manual salting code.
